course: dictionary
lesson: 5060
title: Insignia: badges that show who you are
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An insignia is a badge, sign or symbol that shows someone's rank, job, group or authority. Think of the stripes on a soldier's uniform or the crown on a king's coat of arms. They tell you something about the person without a word being spoken.
explain: The text says, «Russian troops without insignia (badges) seized Crimea.» The brackets give you the meaning: insignia means badges. Without badges or other markings, it was harder to tell whose soldiers they were.
explain: Insignia can also mean the emblem of a club, a scout group or an army, such as its logo. Insignia can be singular or plural, so one insignia and many insignia are both correct.
fact: Insignia means a badge, sign or symbol that shows rank, job, group or authority.
fact: Rank insignia shows how senior a soldier, police officer or scout leader is.
fact: Royal insignia, such as a crown or sceptre, shows the authority of a king or queen.
fact: An emblem is an insignia of a club, army or organisation, such as its logo.
fact: «Insignia» can be singular or plural, and an old singular form is «insigne».
exercise: Soldiers wear insignia on their sleeves to show their rank. What is insignia? | *A badge or sign of rank | A type of weapon | A kind of cloth | A song
exercise: Which of these is an example of royal insignia? | *A crown | A pair of boots | A school bag | A cup of tea
exercise: «Russian troops without insignia (badges) seized Crimea.» Find and copy one word that is a synonym for insignia in the brackets. | troops | seized | *badges | Crimea
test: What does the word insignia mean? | A type of dance | *A badge or symbol showing rank, job, group or authority | A kind of weather | A question
test: Which statement about insignia is true? | It is always a verb | It means a loud noise | *It can be singular or plural | It can only mean one badge
exam: In the sentence «Russian troops without insignia (badges) seized Crimea», why might the missing insignia matter? | Because the sentence is about a school trip | So the soldiers could fly | *So people could not easily tell who the soldiers were or whose side they were on | Because badges are heavy to carry
